==German==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|līden}}, from {{inh|de|goh|līdan}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*līþaną}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|lijden}}, {{cog|en|lithe}}. The sense developed from “go, travel” via “endure” to “suffer”. This happened under the influence of the originally unrelated noun {{m|de|Leid|t=sorrow}}, which is cognate with {{cog|en|loath}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈlaɪ̯dən/|[ˈlaɪ̯dn̩]|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-leiden.ogg|audio|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-strong|class=1|leidet|litt|gelitten|past subjunctive=litte}}

# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[bear]]; to [[endure]]; to [[undergo]] (some hardship)
#: {{ux|de|Lerne '''leiden''' ohne zu klagen.|Learn '''to suffer''' without complaining.}}
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[suffer]]; to feel [[pain]]
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[suffer]] (from; a disease)
#* '''2012''' April 20, ''{{w|Die Welt}}'' [http://epaper.apps.welt.de/welt/archiv/], page 22:
#*: Durch Passivrauchen steigt bei Kindern das Risiko, dass sie als Erwachsene an einer chronisch-obstruktiven Lungenerkrankung '''leiden'''.
#*:: By passive smoking, the risk increases in children that they suffer from chronic obstructive lung disease as adults.

====Conjugation====
{{de-conj-strong|leid|litt|gelitten|h|e|||a}}

====Derived terms====
* [[durchleiden]]
* [[erleiden]]
* [[Leiden]]
* [[Leidenschaft]]
* [[mitleiden]]
* [[verleiden]]

====Related terms====
* [[Leid]] {{q|this and the following are related by secondary association only}}
* [[leid]]
* [[leider]]

